I’m picturing a city planner with a stack of maps who has to decide which pieces of road to upgrade so each little neighborhood group can reach itself easily. The choice is which stretches to fix, knowing the bill is just the total price of all the upgraded roads — so cheaper choices are better as long as every house in each neighborhood cluster can travel to every other house in that same cluster by those upgraded streets. Different neighborhood groups don’t need to be linked together, and upgrading the same road twice doesn’t change the cost — the prices of the chosen road segments are simply added up. The concrete map and costs are shown below.

{{INSTANCE_INPUT}}

Also, when you hand me the chosen streets, please stick to this simple JSON shape — it’s just a tidy checklist of which road segments to upgrade:

{
  "solution": [[u1, v1], [u2, v2], ...]
}

Here’s what that means in plain terms: "solution" is the list of road segments you want to pick. Each inner pair [u1, v1] is one street between node u1 and node v1 — basically, the two endpoints of that stretch. Think of it like filling in boxes on a form: one pair per chosen road.

This JSON is just the sketch of the shape I expect, not the actual final choice. All identifiers you use must match exactly the ones in the instance input — no renaming and no new labels. 

For example: Valid identifiers look like plain numbers such as “1” or “23”, single capital letters like “A” or “B”, or a capital letter followed by digits like “A1” or “X7”.

